    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "My apologies for the confusion. To differentiate between root rot and nitrogen deficiency, look at the pattern of the yellowing leaves. If the yellowing is more pronounced at the bottom of the plant and the leaves are wilting, it could be a sign of root rot, often caused by overwatering. Root rot will also cause the leaves to feel soft and spongy when gently squeezed. On the other hand, if the yellowing is more uniform across the leaves and the plant is still upright, it might be a nitrogen deficiency. In this case, the leaves may also appear smaller than normal and the plant might exhibit stunted growth. To address root rot, you should remove the plant from its pot, trim away any rotten roots, and repot it in fresh, well-draining soil. For nitrogen deficiency, you can apply a balanced fertilizer to provide the necessary nutrients. Just as a goblin would need to adjust its diet to stay healthy, your Monstera will need the right care to recover.",
